Z||000000||ponya||05-09-2000||09:30 AM||||I have an 86 SE that someone had taken the<BR>EGR valve and all its components off the car.<BR>All the components have been replaced, but I still get a check engine light after driving about 5 miles. Just changed out the egr solenoid valve again, and I still get the light. Any suggestions appreciated.<BR>86 SE 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||05-09-2000||10:42 AM||||Did you pull the code from the ECM? What code do you get?||||reg||1
Z||000002||ponya||05-09-2000||11:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>Did you pull the code from the ECM? What code do you get?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I get a Code 32 - this problem is driving my son and I nuts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wxxo||05-09-2000||12:11 PM||||ive got the same problem. i changed out the egr and the light still comes on. i did however figure out how to fix it though. its really easy and cheap. what you have to do is get a short piece of black tape (electric tape will work just fine) and cut it about 1/2 in ch long. make sure that it is exactly 1/2 inch long. its very important to get the measurements right. after that go into the passenger compartment and carefully loewr the steering wheel... and put it over the service enging soon light. <BR>that will fix not only the egr problem but any other problem that can show up on that damn annoying little orange light!! i hate that light!! i hate it hate it hate it!!<P>hope this helped : )||||reg||12
Z||000004||jfuredy||05-09-2000||01:15 PM||||Your problem is probably in your EGR solenoid.  And you do want to fix it because when this code sets it alters the amount of advance that your car uses, thus reducing power.<P>To test the EGR solenoid get a vacuum pump with a gauge on it and connect it to the EGR solenoid port that goes to the manifold.  With the car off, the EGR solenoid should not let you hold a vacuum, and the diaphragm on the underside of the EGR valve should not move.  Now, turn the car to "run" and jumper the diagnostic port (like you do for timing) and repeat the vacuum test.  It should now hold about 5 inches of mercury and the EGR valve's diaphragm should pull up.<P>If the EGR doesn't move when the jumpered then you probably have a short in the solenoids coil.  Check the resistance across the bottom two (i think they were the bottom ones) pins of the EGR solenoid when it's in the car.  The resistance should be about 100 ohms.  The two that I repaired were showing about 1,000,000 ohms.<P>Some day I'll get some pictures of a disassembled solenoid.<P>Joe Furedy||||reg||1
Z||000005||dman||05-09-2000||02:46 PM||||If you have...then this would sound like a silly question: Did you -clear- the old code from the ECM? You need to disconnect the battery for a little bit (30 secs or a minute). This will erase the old code 32. If you didn't, the computer may still show the old code even though you've fixed the problem. Don't forget that disconnecting the battery will cause the ECM to go into a learn mode for little while later.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gokart||05-09-2000||09:16 PM||||I feel your pain Ponya! wxxo's been there too! I've been trying to pinpoint my 32 woes for about 6 months now. I try not to loose sleep over that engine light...but I do! Means she's not running right and I won't give up till she is. Got a brand new solenoid and the valve passes the test. I was told though that I should replace the valve just because of the amount of carbon that has been probably built up over the years. Will install this weekend and will post results. One other thing is the tube that goes from the plenum to the valve...make sure it's not broken/cracked. I had a serious carbon build up where it meets the plenum about the size of a small rock. When I cleaned it out she did run much better. Good luck.||||reg||8
Z||000007||triker||05-10-2000||04:00 PM||||When you get a code 32 it means that the ECM isn't getting a signal from the EGR solenoid saying it's letting vacumn through to the EGR when it should.  You can remove the EGR completely and the ECM wouldn't know the dif.  Take the short 90 degree vac line off the little square box on the solenoid module and hook the vac line from the manifold to it.  If your SES light doesn't come on anymore, the problem is the solenoid.  If the light still comes on, it's  probably the switch in the little box.  Reset your ECM and check for codes in a few days - viola! Nada!  I've had 2 go bad on me and until I run accross another solenoid somewhere, I just leave mine in the 'fool the ECM' mode.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gokart||05-10-2000||09:07 PM||||If code 32 has nothing to do with the valve then how can I STILL be getting that code with a brand new $90.00 solenoid from the Fiero Store last month?||||reg||5
Z||000009||Fiasco||05-10-2000||11:51 PM||||Cracked vac. line? I know it's prob. too obvious and I'm sure everyone has already checked...  but those brittle plastic lines only get more brittle with age in a hot engine compartment. And don't forget the boots. NAPA has this crap, or can get it, anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gokart||05-11-2000||07:12 AM||||My lines are being held together in some places by fuel line hose by prvious owner, but everything is connected. Can't wait for Rodney Dickman's under the plenum vaccum line replacement kit. What code will set off then if the vavle is shot or the tube is cracked/broken?||||reg||1
Z||000011||triker||05-11-2000||01:04 PM||||ponya, there arn't any sensors on the EGR itself, only at the solenoid, But if you don't have vacumn to the solenoid,it will give you code 32 nomatter what the condition of the solenoid.  I will back up and say if you have a vacumn leak anywhere in the circuit, including the EGR, you will get a code 32. What I'm getting at is, that clogged up EGR's, plugged or cracked crossover tubes or even removed EGR valves, won't cause a code 32.||||reg||1
